The Schwarzschild radius \( R_s \) for a black hole is given by the formula:

\[ R_s = \frac{2GM}{c^2} \]

where:
- \( G \) is the gravitational constant (\( 6.6743 \times 10^{-11} \, \text{m}^3 \, \text{kg}^{-1} \, \text{s}^{-2} \)),
- \( M \) is the mass of the black hole,
- \( c \) is the speed of light (\( 2.998 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s} \)).

Given that the mass of the Sun \( M_{\odot} \) is approximately \( 1.989 \times 10^{30} \, \text{kg} \), the mass of the black hole is \( 3M_{\odot} \):

\[ M = 3 \times 1.989 \times 10^{30} \, \text{kg} = 5.967 \times 10^{30} \, \text{kg} \]

First, calculate \( 2GM \):

\[ 2GM = 2 \times 6.6743 \times 10^{-11} \, \text{m}^3 \, \text{kg}^{-1} \, \text{s}^{-2} \times 5.967 \times 10^{30} \, \text{kg} \]

\[ 2GM = 1.33486 \times 10^{-10} \times 5.967 \times 10^{30} \]

\[ 2GM = 7.965 \times 10^{20} \, \text{m}^3 \, \text{s}^{-2} \]

Next, calculate \( c^2 \):

\[ c^2 = (2.998 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s})^2 = 8.987 \times 10^{16} \, \text{m}^2 \, \text{s}^{-2} \]

Now, compute the Schwarzschild radius \( R_s \):

\[ R_s = \frac{7.965 \times 10^{20} \, \text{m}^3 \, \text{s}^{-2}}{8.987 \times 10^{16} \, \text{m}^2 \, \text{s}^{-2}} \]

\[ R_s = 8.86 \times 10^3 \, \text{m} \]

Therefore, the value of \( X \) is approximately 8.9.

